  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Choose one next planning step using the local decision below. Return exactly one listed statement and stop. No other planning choices have been supplied.\n{\"context\": \"Requested books can be grouped on several shelves, and labels can show either a reader name or a pickup window.\", \"decision\": \"How should shelf labels help staff locate requested books?\", \"goal\": \"Keep books moving and make the afternoon service easier to use without excluding readers who arrive without an appointment.\", \"problem\": \"A small library becomes crowded during afternoon pickups and returns. It has a request sheet, adjustable pickup windows, a few trained helpers, labeled shelves, and enough room to separate several activities. Staff want a practical plan that preserves walk-in access. Each decision below concerns a different part of the service; none requires exclusive use of all helpers or space.\"}\nCANDIDATE STATEMENTS:\nStaff group requested books by reader name and add pickup windows within each shelf label. The name grouping narrows the search while the window remains visible.\nStaff group requested books by pickup window and add reader names within each shelf label. The time grouping narrows the search before staff check a name.\nStaff assign a shelf location to each requested book and put that location beside both reader name and pickup window on a desk list. The desk list directs the search without sorting the shelves by either field."
    }
  ],
